# GETUID(2)

## NAME

getuid, getgid, geteuid, getegid - get user and group identity

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <unistd.h>

uid_t getuid(void);

uid_t geteuid(void);

gid_t getgid(void);

gid_t getegid(void);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Getuid* returns the real user ID of the current process, *geteuid* the effective user ID. The
real user ID identifies the person who is logged in, in contradistinction to the effective user
ID, which determines his access permission at the moment. It is thus useful to programs which
operate using the `set user ID` mode, to find out who invoked them.

*Getgid* returns the real group ID, *getegid* the effective group ID.

## SEE ALSO

setuid(2), getpid(2)

## BESM-6 NOTES

**Two extracodes, four entry points.** Each gate returns the real id in the accumulator and the
effective one in r12, so each pair is two labels on one hand-written stub. See intro(2).

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_getuid, SYS_getgid** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it
reads them: 1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
